  "details": "<p>The earliest textual evidence for the name of the wall comes from the reign of the Babylonian king Adad-apla-iddina (<span>1068-1047 B.C.). Excavations at Nippur clearly shows that a city wall\u00a0</span>existed as early as the Early Dynastic Period. Although parts of the Nippur's wall have been excavated, no <span>visible traces of it can be seen in\u00a0</span>satellite imagery (Corona or modern) and, therefore, no coordinates for it can be provided with any degree of certainty.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "The city wall of Nippur according to the so-called \"Kuyunjik Ziqqurrat List\" (7th century B.C.). Its Akkadian ceremonial name means \"The God Marduk Showed Favor.\"",
